#import "_brand.typ": *

#show: letterhead.with(
  dateline: "9 September 2026",
  recipient: [
    CES Media Credentialing \
    Consumer Technology Association
  ],
  subject: [Media credential request — Dr Sean McKnight, MD, Contributing Editor],
)

Dear Credentialing Team,

I am writing on behalf of *The Sports Page* to request media credentials for
*Dr Sean McKnight, MD*, a contributing editor of this publication, who will
be attending CES on assignment for us.

A word about who we are, since we are not a name you will recognise. *The Sports
Page* is a daily statistics newspaper: one strange or counterintuitive number
from the week's sport, explained. We publish Monday through Saturday, and on
Sundays we grade our own published forecasts in public — including the ones we
got wrong. That record stands today at *165 consecutive issues* since Opening
Day of the 2026 baseball season, with *27 companion primers* on the methods
behind them. The whole archive is open at thesportspage.net.

Dr McKnight writes for us on measurement — the gap between what a number is taken
to mean and what it was built to measure. His most recent column, "209 of Every
1,000 Never Pitch Again," ran on 8 September and showed how the reported success
rate of a common orthopaedic operation falls from ninety per cent to well under
half depending entirely on how *success* is defined. He is a practising
physician; that piece is a fair sample of what he brings us.

= His assignment at CES

His brief is *sports and health measurement technology*, and he will be asking
the question this newspaper asks of every figure it prints: what is this device
actually measuring, and does that measurement mean what the marketing says it
means?

Wearables, biometric monitoring, motion capture, sleep and recovery platforms all
produce numbers that reach the public wearing the clothes of fact. Very few
arrive with an error bar or a stated method. That gap is our entire editorial
subject, and CES is the one venue where the whole industry can be examined in a
week rather than a year.

He will file written coverage for *The Sports Page* and produce accompanying
video. We would gladly share the resulting work with your team, and just as
gladly answer any question about the publication or verify his assignment
directly.

Thank you for considering it. We know credentials are finite and that you field
far more requests than you can grant.

#signoff(
  "Patrick E. McKnight, PhD",
  "Owner and Managing Editor, The Sports Page",
  "ideas" + "@" + "thesportspage.net  ·  thesportspage.net",
)

#colophon[
  Every issue of The Sports Page states its data sources and its limits.
  Corrections run under their own dated heading — most recently on 8 September,
  when we retracted a claim in our own Sunday edition. The full archive,
  including our public prediction scorecard, is open to inspection.
]
